{"author":{"id":"6d85cf2fcebaeefd8c5c528f2d5f5f98f26ce3275422d0dc5f7c0fd596f9f59f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791711394,"nonce":"kCFfhqbskdvECeQRjB1lgsNx++I=","title":"Visible glyphs render differently once a process has prepared many text runs","message":"Found while checking 5d7e308 against main. A process renders a page differently after rendering about 26 other pages first than a fresh process renders it. The difference is glyph edges: about 9,000 pixels per 1120x780 frame, up to 157 levels per channel, and a column of antialiasing shifted on headings. The fresh render is the right one.\n\nReproduce with blinc_ts's wiki:\n1. Render every page, in order, at four scroll depths, in one process.\n2. Render one page (e.g. Motion) in a fresh process.\n3. Hash the frames. At main 75dc66f they differ from the Motion page on. With 5d7e308's culling the static pages match.\n\nThe culling avoids it only by shaping far fewer runs; the cause is still there. Suspects, in the order to check:\n- The bounded glyph LRU (GLYPH_CACHE_CAPACITY) or the text layout cache (256 entries) returns an entry made for a different subpixel phase or raster scale, its key missing one of them.\n- An atlas epoch or reset leaves stale UV rects in a cached prepared layout.\n\nA long session in an app with much text would drift the same way. A test should render one run fresh, then after preparing enough distinct runs to cycle both caches, and compare the glyph records and atlas pixels.","files":null}]}